IMPORT daydate

-- How `l4 nlg` writes two literals.
--
-- A percentage reads `50%`, glued to its number. It used to read `50 %` with a
-- space before the sign and another after it, because the number and a
-- punctuation `%` were joined with a space and the punctuation carries its own
-- trailing space.
#EVAL 200 TIMES 50%

-- A call to daydate's `Date` on three whole-number literals reads as the date
-- it names, in either spelling of the constructor.
#EVAL Date 16 7 2025
#EVAL `Days to date` 1 1 2026

-- Every miss falls through to the ordinary call, unchanged.
--
-- A component that is not a literal: a variable has no date to print.
GIVEN day IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the july day` MEANS Date day 7 2025

#EVAL `the july day` 16

-- A date that does not exist. `Date` rolls an out-of-range component forward
-- (31 February becomes March), so printing it as a calendar date would state a
-- day that is not in the text.
#EVAL Date 31 2 2025

-- A literal that is not whole.
#EVAL Date 16.5 7 2025
